f:["$","$L16",null,{"guide":{"date":"July 1, 1862","place":"Malvern Hill, an open plateau north of the James River in Henrico County, Virginia, about fifteen miles southeast of Richmond","result":"This episode establishes the terrain, opposing deployments, and Confederate plan before the main artillery and infantry fighting. The Army of the Potomac occupied a compact position supported by massed artillery, while Robert E. Lee planned to suppress the Federal guns with artillery on two ridges and then attack. Misidentified roads, dispersed batteries, ammunition shortages, and an ambiguous attack order prevented the intended Confederate concentration. The full battle, covered in the following episode, ended in a United States victory","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The battlefield and command arrangements before the main fighting at Malvern Hill","note":"The narrative stops after explaining the failed Confederate artillery concentration. The gun duel, infantry assaults, and casualty totals belong to the following episode."},{"label":"Campaign setting","value":"Final battle of the Seven Days, one day after Glendale","note":"By July 1, the Army of the Potomac had concentrated on Malvern Hill during its movement toward the James River."},{"label":"Battlefield form","value":"An elevated, open plateau with long fields of fire","note":"The episode describes the plateau as about one and one-quarter miles north to south, three-quarters of a mile wide, and approximately 130 feet above the James River. Published measurements vary according to the boundaries used."},{"label":"Natural boundaries","value":"Turkey Run and Western Run framed the plateau","note":"Malvern Cliffs and Turkey Run protected the western side. Wooded, swampy Western Run bordered the east and crossed the northern approach below the open slope."},{"label":"Main approach","value":"Willis Church Road crossed the plateau from north to south","note":"The road divided the principal Federal position and became the central reference line for both armies."},{"label":"Federal formation","value":"A loose inverted horseshoe facing north and east","note":"Porter’s V Corps held the western and northern sectors; Couch’s division extended east of Willis Church Road. Sumner and Heintzelman stood behind the eastern side, and Franklin guarded approaches nearer the James."},{"label":"V Corps frontage","value":"Sykes west of Willis Church Road; Morell across the northern face","note":"Morell’s division curved around the Crew house. Griffin held the forward field, with Martindale and Butterfield behind and to the east."},{"label":"Couch’s deployment","value":"Three brigades in depth near the West house","note":"The episode identifies the brigades of Innis N. Palmer, Albion P. Howe, and John J. Abercrombie. Palmer’s understrength brigade formed the forward line."},{"label":"Federal artillery","value":"Army artillery and siege guns were available around the plateau","note":"The episode gives 268 field pieces and 26 siege guns in the vicinity. It places 31 guns in seven batteries on the northern front, about 60 guns in ten batteries to the east, and roughly 50 more guns in reserve. These figures describe availability and deployment areas, not simultaneous fire from one line."},{"label":"Artillery direction","value":"Col. Henry J. Hunt organized and sustained the Federal gun line","note":"As Army of the Potomac chief of artillery, Hunt selected positions and arranged for batteries to rotate through the firing line."},{"label":"McClellan’s movements","value":"Inspected Harrison’s Landing before returning to Malvern Hill in mid-afternoon","note":"McClellan traveled from Haxall’s Landing aboard USS Galena and later remained on the eastern side of the Federal position. Porter directed the sector facing the principal Confederate approach."},{"label":"Lee’s condition","value":"Fatigued and ill after the preceding campaign days","note":"The episode reports that Lee asked Longstreet to remain with him in case Longstreet needed to assume command. The extent to which fatigue affected later decisions remains an interpretation rather than a measurable fact."},{"label":"D. H. Hill’s warning","value":"Advised against attacking if McClellan held Malvern Hill in strength","note":"Hill had questioned a local chaplain about the ground and relayed the warning to Lee near Willis Methodist Church."},{"label":"Confederate assumption","value":"Lee and Longstreet believed the retreating Federal army was demoralized","note":"D. H. Hill later identified this belief as a reason Lee risked an attack against a strong position."},{"label":"Intended Confederate alignment","value":"Jackson east of Willis Church Road; Magruder west of it","note":"Lee selected commands that had done little fighting at Glendale: Jackson, Huger, and Magruder."},{"label":"Road-name error","value":"Lee called Willis Church Road the Quaker Road","note":"Confederate maps applied the Quaker Road name to Willis Church Road, while local guides knew a different Quaker Road about two miles west. The mismatch sent Magruder away from his intended route until an aide redirected him."},{"label":"Unmapped eastern road","value":"A parallel road east of Willis Church Road did not appear on Lee’s maps","note":"The episode notes that Federal wagons had used it and that it might have offered Jackson a better flanking approach. That possible use is counterfactual and cannot be verified as an available attack route under battlefield conditions."},{"label":"Longstreet’s proposal","value":"Mass artillery on both sides of Willis Church Road to create converging fire","note":"Longstreet estimated that about 60 guns could fit on the plateau behind Armistead and Wright. Lee selected the Poindexter farm ridge for the corresponding position on the eastern side."},{"label":"Armistead’s role","value":"His advance was named as the signal for a general infantry attack","note":"The order said that if artillery broke the Federal line, Armistead was to charge with a yell and other commands were to do the same. Armistead did not receive the order because it went only to division commanders."},{"label":"Chilton order","value":"Undated and dependent on an audible yell across the battlefield","note":"Lee’s aide Robert H. Chilton drafted the order before 1 p.m. Its wording did not specify how commanders were to verify that the bombardment had broken the Federal line."},{"label":"Confederate artillery problem","value":"The two planned concentrations were never assembled","note":"Available batteries were dispersed among divisions, routes and orders were unclear, and several batteries never reached the intended ridges."},{"label":"D. H. Hill’s batteries","value":"Seven batteries lacked ammunition after White Oak Swamp","note":"The episode states that Hill’s artillery had exhausted its ammunition during the June 30 bombardment and was unavailable for the planned Malvern Hill concentration."},{"label":"Artillery reserve","value":"Six batteries under Brig. Gen. William N. Pendleton remained idle","note":"The episode finds no record of Lee summoning the reserve. Pendleton searched for Lee but did not establish contact in time to employ the batteries."},{"label":"Battle result outside episode scope","value":"United States victory","note":"The National Park Service estimates 8,355 casualties for the complete battle: about 3,000 United States and 5,355 Confederate. These losses occurred during fighting narrated in the following episode, not during this episode’s setup."},{"label":"Senior leader losses in this episode","value":"None","note":"No senior commander is killed or mortally wounded during the preparatory events covered here."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“Voices of the Civil War: The Seven Days” by the editors of Time-Life Books","note":"The episode recommends this collection of participant accounts, and the official podcast listing confirms the title."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Morell, Couch, Heintzelman, Huger, Haxall’s Landing, Abercrombie, Poindexter, and Chilton","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “Morrill,” “Daria’s couches,” “Heinzelman,” “UJ,” “Haxle’s,” “Ambercrombie,” and “Presthard.”"}],"maps":[{"title":"Malvern Hill in the Peninsula Campaign","caption":"The Army of the Potomac advanced from Fort Monroe toward Richmond between the York and James rivers, then moved southeast during the Seven Days toward the security of the James. Malvern Hill stood between Glendale and Harrison’s Landing. Locations, shorelines, routes, and distances are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"U.S. Army Center of Military History, National Park Service, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep166-peninsula-context.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map of eastern Virginia showing Richmond, the Virginia Peninsula, Fort Monroe, Yorktown, Glendale, Malvern Hill, Harrison’s Landing, and the Army of the Potomac’s broad movement."},{"title":"Positions and intended artillery plan at Malvern Hill","caption":"Federal infantry and artillery occupied the plateau around the Crew and West houses. Lee intended Confederate artillery on the Armistead ridge west of Willis Church Road and the Poindexter ridge east of it to subject the Federal line to converging fire before an infantry assault. Magruder initially moved toward the actual Quaker Road after Lee used that name for Willis Church Road. Unit areas and arrows show generalized relationships, not exact routes, formations, frontage, or gun positions. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Historic American Landscapes Survey, Robert Knox Sneden maps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep166-malvern-hill-positions.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Malvern Hill showing the Federal line, Sykes, Morell, Couch, supporting corps, the planned Confederate artillery ridges, Jackson, Huger, Magruder’s mistaken movement, Willis Church Road, Quaker Road, Turkey Run, Western Run, and the James River."},{"title":"Alfred Waud’s battlefield drawing","caption":"Alfred R. Waud made this drawing at Malvern Hill on July 1, 1862. It is a participant-era visual record of the battle rather than a surveyed map of the positions.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep166-malvern-hill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud pencil and Chinese white drawing of United States artillery and troops at the Battle of Malvern Hill."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:09","audioTime":69,"title":"Glendale is reviewed","detail":"The episode begins by assessing why Lee’s June 30 plan failed to trap the retreating Federal army."},{"time":"2:52","audioTime":172,"title":"Command failures at Glendale are assigned","detail":"Huger and Jackson are criticized, but the episode places primary army-level responsibility on Lee."},{"time":"4:20","audioTime":260,"title":"Lee’s June 30 inspection of Malvern Hill is recalled","detail":"Lee had examined Holmes’s sector and diverted Magruder toward the River Road before reversing part of that movement."},{"time":"7:28","audioTime":448,"title":"The Army of the Potomac concentrates","detail":"On July 1, the Federal army is assembled at Malvern Hill with the James River behind it."},{"time":"8:05","audioTime":485,"title":"The plateau is described","detail":"The episode gives the dimensions and elevation of the open ground above the James."},{"time":"8:27","audioTime":507,"title":"The watercourses frame the position","detail":"Turkey Run, Western Run, and the Malvern Cliffs define the principal approaches and flanks."},{"time":"9:24","audioTime":564,"title":"Porter evaluates the defensive ground","detail":"Porter considers Malvern Hill better suited to defense than earlier V Corps positions."},{"time":"9:39","audioTime":579,"title":"The Federal line is outlined","detail":"The army forms a loose inverted horseshoe facing the expected Confederate approach."},{"time":"9:51","audioTime":591,"title":"Sykes and Morell take the principal front","detail":"Sykes covers the western cliffs and River Road while Morell spans the northern face toward Willis Church Road."},{"time":"10:48","audioTime":648,"title":"Couch extends the line east","detail":"Couch places Palmer, Howe, and Abercrombie in depth near the West house."},{"time":"11:19","audioTime":679,"title":"The supporting corps are located","detail":"Sumner and Heintzelman stand behind the eastern side, with Franklin nearer the James."},{"time":"11:34","audioTime":694,"title":"Federal artillery becomes the central defensive advantage","detail":"The episode inventories the field batteries, siege guns, and reserve pieces available around Malvern Hill."},{"time":"12:27","audioTime":747,"title":"Hunt organizes the gun line","detail":"Henry J. Hunt places batteries and prepares to rotate them to sustain the fire."},{"time":"12:44","audioTime":764,"title":"McClellan leaves for the James","detail":"After visiting the lines, McClellan goes to Haxall’s Landing and inspects Harrison’s Landing aboard USS Galena."},{"time":"13:13","audioTime":793,"title":"McClellan returns in mid-afternoon","detail":"He comes back to Malvern Hill and remains on the army’s eastern flank while Porter directs the northern sector."},{"time":"15:04","audioTime":904,"title":"The position’s narrow front is assessed","detail":"The episode notes that only a limited number of defenders could occupy the 1,200-yard northern front at once, but nearby reserves could respond to a breakthrough."},{"time":"16:16","audioTime":976,"title":"Lee’s condition is considered","detail":"Lee is described as fatigued and ill after days of limited sleep and operational disappointment."},{"time":"17:26","audioTime":1046,"title":"D. H. Hill warns against an attack","detail":"Near Willis Methodist Church, Hill reports local information about the strength of the ground and advises leaving McClellan alone if he holds it in force."},{"time":"18:11","audioTime":1091,"title":"Longstreet judges the Federal army demoralized","detail":"Longstreet interprets the continuing retreat as evidence that the Army of the Potomac can be defeated by another attack."},{"time":"18:59","audioTime":1139,"title":"Jackson crosses White Oak Swamp","detail":"Jackson moves south through Glendale and meets Magruder before both commanders report to Lee."},{"time":"19:30","audioTime":1170,"title":"Lee assigns the approach columns","detail":"Jackson is to move east of Willis Church Road and Magruder is to follow and deploy west of it."},{"time":"19:44","audioTime":1184,"title":"The road-name error redirects Magruder","detail":"Lee’s use of “Quaker Road” for Willis Church Road leads local guides toward the actual Quaker Road to the west."},{"time":"20:40","audioTime":1240,"title":"Lee and Longstreet inspect opposite sides","detail":"Longstreet scouts west of Willis Church Road while Lee examines the eastern approaches."},{"time":"21:03","audioTime":1263,"title":"Longstreet identifies an artillery ridge","detail":"He finds Armistead and Wright on high ground north of the Federal line and proposes massing about 60 guns there."},{"time":"22:11","audioTime":1331,"title":"Magruder’s mistaken movement is discovered","detail":"Longstreet sees Magruder’s troops moving away from the intended battlefield route and reports the problem to Lee."},{"time":"22:44","audioTime":1364,"title":"Lee approves the crossfire concept","detail":"Lee accepts Longstreet’s proposal, sends an aide to redirect Magruder, and expects batteries to assemble on the western ridge."},{"time":"23:14","audioTime":1394,"title":"Lee selects the Poindexter ridge","detail":"The eastern ridge near the Poindexter farm is chosen for the second planned artillery concentration."},{"time":"23:48","audioTime":1428,"title":"Chilton drafts the attack order","detail":"The order makes a break in the Federal line and Armistead’s charge with a yell the conditions for other commands to advance."},{"time":"26:00","audioTime":1560,"title":"Armistead does not receive the order","detail":"Copies go to division commanders, leaving the brigadier named as the signal unaware of that role."},{"time":"26:23","audioTime":1583,"title":"The artillery concentration fails","detail":"The episode identifies inadequate numbers, dispersed batteries, ammunition shortages, and poor communication."},{"time":"27:08","audioTime":1628,"title":"D. H. Hill’s batteries are unavailable","detail":"Seven batteries have exhausted their ammunition at White Oak Swamp, while Jackson’s remaining batteries are scattered among divisions."},{"time":"27:26","audioTime":1646,"title":"Pendleton’s reserve remains idle","detail":"The six-battery artillery reserve receives no effective direction and does not join the planned concentration."},{"time":"28:35","audioTime":1715,"title":"The book recommendation begins","detail":"The hosts recommend “Voices of the Civil War: The Seven Days” by the editors of Time-Life Books."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Selected Harrison’s Landing as the army’s destination; returned to Malvern Hill in mid-afternoon and remained on the eastern flank"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter","command":"V Corps and principal northern defense","subordinates":"Divisions of Brig. Gens. George W. Morell and George Sykes"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George W. Morell","command":"V Corps division on the northern face","subordinates":"Brigades of Charles Griffin, John H. Martindale, and Daniel Butterfield around the Crew house and Willis Church Road"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George Sykes","command":"V Corps division on the western sector","subordinates":"Covered Malvern Cliffs and the River Road approaches"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Darius N. Couch","command":"IV Corps division east of Willis Church Road","subordinates":"Brigades of Innis N. Palmer, Albion P. Howe, and John J. Abercrombie near the West house"},{"commander":"Maj. Gens. Edwin V. Sumner and Samuel P. Heintzelman","command":"II and III Corps","subordinates":"Supported the eastern side of the main position"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. William B. Franklin","command":"VI Corps","subordinates":"Guarded the eastern River Road approaches nearer the James"},{"commander":"Col. Henry J. Hunt","command":"Chief of artillery, Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Selected gun positions and organized the rotation of batteries"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Directed the approach and approved the plan for artillery concentrations on both sides of Willis Church Road"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Command east of Willis Church Road","subordinates":"Included his own division and divisions under D. H. Hill, Richard S. Ewell, and W. H. C. Whiting"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John B. Magruder","command":"Command intended for the west side of Willis Church Road","subordinates":"Initially followed local guides toward the actual Quaker Road before Lee’s aide redirected the column"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Benjamin Huger","command":"Division approaching the western sector","subordinates":"Brigades under Lewis A. Armistead and Ambrose R. Wright reached the ridge north of Malvern Hill"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Army-level adviser and western reconnaissance","subordinates":"Proposed massing artillery behind Armistead and Wright to fire across the Federal position"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill","command":"Division in Jackson’s column","subordinates":"Warned Lee about the ground; his seven batteries lacked ammunition after White Oak Swamp"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. William N. Pendleton","command":"Army artillery reserve","subordinates":"Six batteries remained unused after Pendleton failed to establish contact with Lee"},{"commander":"Col. Robert H. Chilton","command":"Assistant adjutant general on Lee’s staff","subordinates":"Drafted the order linking a successful bombardment, Armistead’s advance, and a general attack"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who approved the artillery-crossfire plan and the conditional infantry order","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep166-robert-e-lee.jpg"},{"name":"Henry J. Hunt","role":"Army of the Potomac chief of artillery who organized the Federal gun line and battery rotation","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep166-henry-hunt.jpg"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"V Corps commander responsible for the principal northern sector of the Federal defense","side":"United States"},{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander who inspected Harrison’s Landing and returned to the army’s eastern flank","side":"United States"},{"name":"Darius N. Couch","role":"Division commander holding the Federal line east of Willis Church Road","side":"United States"},{"name":"George W. Morell","role":"Division commander holding the northern face of Malvern Hill around the Crew house","side":"United States"},{"name":"George Sykes","role":"Division commander covering Malvern Cliffs and the western approaches","side":"United States"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate division commander who proposed the two-ridge artillery concentration","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"D. H. Hill","role":"Confederate division commander who advised Lee against attacking a strongly held Malvern Hill","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate commander assigned to approach east of Willis Church Road","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John B. Magruder","role":"Confederate commander whose column was misdirected by the Quaker Road naming error","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Lewis A. Armistead","role":"Brigade commander named in Chilton’s order as the intended signal for a general advance","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William N. Pendleton","role":"Confederate artillery reserve commander whose six batteries remained idle","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Robert H. Chilton","role":"Lee’s assistant adjutant general and drafter of the conditional attack order","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep166-peninsula-context.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map of the Virginia Peninsula and the movement from Richmond toward the James River.","caption":"The broad campaign map places Malvern Hill between Glendale and Harrison’s Landing during the Army of the Potomac’s movement away from Richmond.","source":"U.S. Army Center of Military History, National Park Service, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep166-malvern-hill-positions.svg","alt":"Schematic troop-position and movement map for the opening arrangements at Malvern Hill.","caption":"The northern plateau concentrated Porter’s and Couch’s divisions with Federal artillery. The proposed Confederate gun positions stood on separate ridges north of that line, but the planned concentration did not occur.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Historic American Landscapes Survey, Robert Knox Sneden maps, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep166-malvern-hill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of United States guns and troops at Malvern Hill on July 1, 1862.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud drew the Battle of Malvern Hill on July 1, 1862. The original is pencil and Chinese white on tan paper.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep166-henry-hunt.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era photographic portrait of United States artillery officer Henry J. Hunt.","caption":"Henry J. Hunt, photographed during the Civil War, served as the Army of the Potomac’s chief of artillery and organized the Malvern Hill batteries.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep166-robert-e-lee.jpg","alt":"1864 photographic portrait of Confederate general Robert E. Lee in uniform.","caption":"Julian Vannerson photographed Robert E. Lee in March 1864. Lee commanded the Confederate army at Malvern Hill in 1862.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 166 listing","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/10/02/civil-war-podcast-episode-166/"},{"label":"Richmond National Battlefield Park: The Battle of Malvern Hill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/mhbull.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service Civil War battle summary: Malvern Hill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va021"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"Library of Congress Historic American Landscapes Survey: Battle of Malvern Hill","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/va2114/"},{"label":"Primary map: Robert Knox Sneden, revised plan of Malvern Hill, July 1, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00034/"},{"label":"Primary map: Robert Knox Sneden, plan of Malvern Hill, June 30 and July 1, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00252/"},{"label":"Primary visual source: Alfred R. Waud’s Malvern Hill drawing, July 1, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660457/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Henry J. Hunt portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666441/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Robert E. Lee portrait by Julian Vannerson","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"}]}}]
